You could use uniVocity-parsers for faster and more efficient column renaming in CSV files without loading everything into memory. Here's a simplified example:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
    Writer output = new StringWriter();
    CsvWriterSettings writerSettings = new CsvWriterSettings();
    final CsvWriter writer = new CsvWriter(output, writerSettings);

    CsvParserSettings parserSettings = new CsvParserSettings();
    parserSettings.setHeaderExtractionEnabled(true);
    parserSettings.setRowProcessor(new AbstractRowProcessor() {
        public void processStarted(ParsingContext context) {
            writer.writeHeaders("Column A", "Column B", "New Column Name");
        }

        public void rowProcessed(String[] row, ParsingContext context) {
            writer.writeRow(row);
        }

        public void processEnded(ParsingContext context) {
            writer.close();
        }
    });

    CsvParser parser = new CsvParser(parserSettings);
    Reader reader = new StringReader("A,B,C\n1,2,3\n4,5,6");
    parser.parse(reader);

    System.out.println(output.toString());
}
```

This code uses uniVocity-parsers to parse the CSV file and rename columns using a row processor.

To add a new section in an INI file using TJvAppIniFileStorage, you can use the following approach:

```delphi
procedure AddNewSection(const IniFileName: string; const SectionName: string);
var
  IniStream: TMemoryStream;
begin
  IniStream := TMemoryStream.Create;
  try
    IniStream.WriteBuffer(Format('%s = %s', [SectionName, '']), Length('[%s'] + #13#10); // Format the section name and add a newline
    TJvAppIniFileStorage.DefaultSection := SectionName; // Set default section to newly added one
    TJvAppIniFileStorage.WriteFromFile(IniStream, IniFileName);
  finally
    IniStream.Free;
  end;
end;
```

This code creates a new section in the INI file by first writing the section name and then setting the `DefaultSection` property of `TJvAppIniFileStorage` to this newly added section.

